## 1. What is a Tokenizer?

A neural network cannot read text. It only works with numbers. A **tokenizer** is the bridge:

```
"Hello world"  --encode-->  [15496, 995]  --model-->  ...  --decode-->  "Hello world"
   text                       token IDs                                     text
```

- **Encode:** text to a list of integer token IDs.
- **Decode:** token IDs back to text.
- **Vocabulary:** the full list of tokens the tokenizer knows (each token has a unique ID).

The typical pipeline:

```
Raw text
   |
   v
1. Normalization      (clean text, e.g. Unicode normalization)
   |
   v
2. Pre-tokenization   (split into chunks: words, numbers, punctuation)
   |
   v
3. Model (e.g. BPE)   (split each chunk into sub-word tokens)
   |
   v
4. Post-processing    (add special tokens like <|endoftext|>)
   |
   v
Token IDs
```

> **Important:** The tokenizer is trained **separately** from the LLM, and it is fixed before LLM training starts. If you change it later, the model's embeddings no longer match.

