## 2. Roadmap: Levels of Tokenization

| Level | Method | Vocab size | Good | Bad |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Character-level | Tiny (~100) | No unknown words | Very long sequences |
| 2 | Word-level | Huge (100k+) | Short sequences | Unknown words (`<unk>`), huge vocab |
| 3 to 4 | Sub-word (BPE) | Medium (32k to 200k) | Best balance | Needs training |
| 4 | Byte-level BPE | 256 + merges | Can encode any text, no unknowns | Slightly longer on rare scripts |
| 5+ | Regex pre-tokenization, special tokens | Same | Cleaner, better tokens | More design choices |

**Why sub-words?** Common words get their own token (`the`), and rare words are split into pieces (`tokenization` becomes `token` + `ization`). This handles new words without a giant vocabulary.

