## 8. Level 6: Special Tokens and Chat Templates

Special tokens are **reserved strings** that are never split and are never learned by BPE. They carry structure.

| Special token | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `<|endoftext|>` | Separates documents during training; signals "stop" during generation |
| `<|pad|>` | Pads shorter sequences in a batch to equal length |
| `<|unk|>` | Unknown token (rarely needed with byte-level BPE) |
| `<|bos|>` / `<|eos|>` | Beginning / end of sequence |
| Role tokens (e.g. `<|user|>`, `<|assistant|>`) | Mark who is speaking in chat models |

Example chat format (yours can differ):

```
<|user|>What is a tokenizer?<|endoftext|><|assistant|>A tokenizer converts text to numbers.<|endoftext|>
```

**Tips:**
- Register special tokens **after** training BPE so they get IDs at the end of the vocabulary.
- Reserve a few **spare tokens** (e.g. `<|reserved_0|>` to `<|reserved_9|>`) so you can add features later without changing the vocabulary size.
- Never let user text create special tokens by accident (for example, a user typing `<|endoftext|>`). Decide whether to encode it as plain text or as the special token, and be consistent.

