## 11. How to Make Your Tokenization Better

"Better" means: **fewer tokens for the same text, meaningful pieces, and fair treatment of every language and domain you care about.**

### 1. Train the tokenizer on data that looks like your target data

A tokenizer trained on English text will split code, math, or Hindi into many tiny pieces. Use a **mix** that matches what your model will see: for example web text, books, code, and your target languages, in the proportions you care about.

### 2. Choose the vocabulary size deliberately

The table below is a **measured experiment** using my code on the GPL-3 license text (trained on the first 25,000 characters, tested on the unseen rest). Bigger vocabulary gives fewer tokens, but with diminishing returns.

| Vocab size | Tokens for test text | Bytes per token (higher = better compression) |
|---|---|---|
| 256 (bytes only) | 10,149 | 1.00 |
| 300 | 7,323 | 1.39 |
| 400 | 5,753 | 1.76 |
| 600 | 4,730 | 2.15 |
| 1000 | 4,060 | 2.50 |

Trade-offs for real models:

| Bigger vocabulary | Smaller vocabulary |
|---|---|
| Shorter sequences (faster attention, more text fits in context) | Longer sequences |
| Larger embedding and output layers (more parameters and memory) | Smaller embedding layers |
| Rare tokens get few training examples and stay poorly learned | Every token is seen often |

For reference, common real-world sizes are roughly 32k (Llama 2), about 50k (GPT-2), about 100k (GPT-4 family), and about 128k (Llama 3). Multilingual models usually need larger vocabularies.

### 3. Design the pre-tokenization regex carefully

The regex decides what can ever be inside one token. Ideas to improve it:

- **Split numbers into individual digits** (`2025` becomes `2`, `0`, `2`, `5`). Some model families do this, and it tends to help arithmetic because numbers are represented consistently. Regex example: replace `\p{N}+` with `\p{N}`.
- **Limit long digit runs** (for example groups of up to 3 digits) as a middle ground.
- **Keep code indentation efficient**: make sure runs of spaces/tabs can become single tokens, otherwise code becomes very expensive.
- **Don't merge across newlines and punctuation** unless you want that.

### 4. Normalize text, but be careful

- Use **Unicode normalization (NFC)** so the same character written in two ways gets the same tokens:

  ```python
  import unicodedata
  text = unicodedata.normalize("NFC", text)
  ```
- Clean control characters and broken encodings.
- Avoid lowercasing for LLMs unless you have a reason: it destroys information like names and code.

### 5. Balance languages fairly

If a language is rare in your tokenizer training data, its text needs many more tokens (higher **fertility**, see next section). That makes it costlier and lower quality. Fix it by **up-sampling** that language when training the tokenizer.

### 6. Clean and deduplicate the training data

Repeated boilerplate (menus, licenses, spam) creates useless tokens. Very rare or odd strings can become "glitch" tokens that the model barely learns. Deduplicate and filter before training the tokenizer.

### 7. Keep byte-level fallback

Always include all 256 bytes so nothing is ever unknown. If you use SentencePiece, turn on `byte_fallback`.

### 8. Plan special tokens early

Add role tokens, padding, and some reserved slots **before** LLM training. Adding new tokens later means resizing embeddings and training them from scratch.

### 9. Pick a vocabulary size that suits your hardware

Round it to a multiple of 64 (for example 32,000 or 50,304). This makes matrix multiplications on GPUs a bit more efficient.

### 10. Evaluate, change one thing, evaluate again

Use the metrics in the next section on a **held-out** dataset, not the training text.

