## 12. How to Evaluate a Tokenizer

| Metric | How to compute | What it tells you |
|---|---|---|
| **Compression (bytes per token)** | `len(text.encode()) / len(tokens)` | Higher means fewer tokens per text |
| **Fertility (tokens per word)** | `num_tokens / num_words` | Lower is better; compare across languages |
| **Round-trip accuracy** | `decode(encode(x)) == x` | Must be 100% |
| **Unknown token rate** | Share of `<unk>` | Should be 0 for byte-level |
| **Vocabulary usage** | Share of tokens actually used on held-out data | Many unused tokens means wasted vocabulary |
| **Speed** | Tokens per second | Matters for large datasets |
| **Downstream performance** | Train small models with different tokenizers and compare validation loss per byte | The real test |

Quick evaluation code:

```python
def evaluate(tok, text):
    ids = tok.encode(text)
    n_bytes = len(text.encode("utf-8"))
    n_words = len(text.split())
    print("tokens          :", len(ids))
    print("bytes per token :", round(n_bytes / len(ids), 2))
    print("tokens per word :", round(len(ids) / n_words, 2))
    print("round trip ok   :", tok.decode(ids) == text)
    print("vocab used      :", f"{len(set(ids))}/{len(tok.vocab)}")

evaluate(tok, open("held_out.txt", encoding="utf-8").read())
```

> **Tip:** When comparing tokenizers with different vocabulary sizes, do not compare raw loss per token. Compare **loss per byte** (or bits per byte), because tokens mean different things in each tokenizer.

