## 15. FAQ

**Q1. Why not just use words as tokens?**
The vocabulary would be huge, and any unseen word would be unknown. Sub-words solve both problems.

**Q2. Why not just use characters?**
Sequences become very long, which is slow and makes learning long-range patterns harder.

**Q3. Why bytes instead of characters?**
There are only 256 bytes but over a million possible Unicode characters. Starting from bytes means the tokenizer can encode anything with no unknown tokens.

**Q4. What is the difference between the tokenizer and the embedding layer?**
The tokenizer turns text into IDs (no learning by gradient descent). The embedding layer turns each ID into a learned vector inside the model.

**Q5. Do I need to train the tokenizer on the same data as the LLM?**
Ideally on the same distribution (a sample of it). It does not need to be all of it.

**Q6. Can I add new words to the tokenizer later?**
You can add tokens, but the model must then learn embeddings for them, which needs more training. It is best to plan the vocabulary in advance.

**Q7. Why do LLMs sometimes struggle to count letters or reverse words?**
The model sees tokens, not letters. `strawberry` may be 2 or 3 tokens, so individual letters are not directly visible to it.

**Q8. What vocabulary size should I start with?**
For a small learning project, 1,000 to 8,000. For a real general-purpose model, 32,000 to 128,000 or more, depending on languages and domains.

**Q9. Is BPE the best algorithm?**
It is the most widely used and works well. Unigram and WordPiece are alternatives with different trade-offs. The training data, regex, and vocabulary size often matter more than the choice of algorithm.

**Q10. Should I write my own tokenizer for a real project?**
Write one to learn. For real projects, use Hugging Face `tokenizers`, `tiktoken`, or `sentencepiece` because they are faster and well tested.

